      "note": "Medicines with a known blood pressure lowering effect. The published examples are alpha-blockers, beta-blockers, nitrates, SGLT2 inhibitors, levodopa and antipsychotics. This draft also lists the usual blood pressure medicines (ACE inhibitors, angiotensin receptor blockers, the listed systemic calcium channel blockers, and diuretics) because they clearly lower blood pressure. Count diltiazem and lercanidipine taken by mouth. Tamsulosin is included as an alpha-blocker. Generic packs that show the ingredient name with a company name count too. Count oral blood pressure medicines and oral clozapine. Count systemic nitrate forms, including sublingual tablets, sprays and transdermal patches. Exclude creams or ointments used for local treatment. Count each distinct qualifying ingredient once, including an ingredient supplied in a combination product."
    }
  ],
  "rule_for_models": "Suspect a finding when the person is aged 65 or over and the record shows a supply of a Group A medicine at a daily dose of 50 mg or more (work out the daily dose as the mg per tablet or capsule times the total number prescribed per day in the directions) or any supply of a Group B medicine, AND at least one Group C medicine, and the supply dates overlap or fall within 60 days of each other. If the other criteria could be met and the Group A dose might reach 50 mg daily, report a possible finding and ask what daily dose is taken. This includes an unclear frequency or a dose range crossing 50 mg. A known maximum below 50 mg does not qualify. One supply of each is enough. Exclude a pair when the record explicitly documents that one medicine stopped before the other began. If age is unknown and the other evidence could meet this criterion, report at most a possible finding and ask the pharmacist to confirm whether the person is aged 65 or over.",
  "cannot_be_seen": "Why each medicine was prescribed, the real blood pressure, the dose really taken, and medicines bought without a prescription or supplied by a hospital or another pharmacy. Ask about lightheadedness or dizziness on standing and recent falls. If the directions are unclear, ask what dose of the tricyclic is taken each day.",
  "ask_the_gp": "A review of the antidepressant and of the blood pressure medicine together, and a check of lying and standing blood pressure.",
  "urgency": "routine",
  "draft": true,
  "signed_by_clinical_reviewer": null
}
